#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299739 :question/problem "Let $\\mathbf{a}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ and $\\mathbf{b}=\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.$ Given that the scalar projection of $\\mathbf{a}$ onto $\\mathbf{b}$ is $0,$ find the value of $x.$\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Since $\\mathbf{a}$ is a nonzero vector, its scalar projection onto another nonzero vector $\\mathbf{b}$ equals zero only when the vectors are perpendicular. \n\nSo, we must have\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{a}⊥\\mathbf{b}\\hspace{2em}⟹\\hspace{2em}\\mathbf{a}\\cdot \\mathbf{b}=0.\n$$\n\nComputing the dot product, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{a}\\cdot \\mathbf{b} & ={a}_{x}\\cdot {b}_{x}+{a}_{y}\\cdot {b}_{y}+{a}_{z}\\cdot {b}_{z} \\\\  & =3\\cdot x+(-2)\\cdot 1+1\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =3x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the dot product equal to $0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{a}\\cdot \\mathbf{b} & =0 \\\\ 3x & =0 \\\\ x & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299740 :question/problem "Let $\\mathbf{a}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ and $\\mathbf{b}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ x \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}.$ Given that the scalar projection of $\\mathbf{a}$ onto $\\mathbf{b}$ is $0,$ find the value of $x.$\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Since $\\mathbf{a}$ is a nonzero vector, its scalar projection onto another nonzero vector $\\mathbf{b}$ equals zero only when the vectors are perpendicular. \n\nSo, we must have\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{a}⊥\\mathbf{b}\\hspace{2em}⟹\\hspace{2em}\\mathbf{a}\\cdot \\mathbf{b}=0.\n$$\n\nComputing the dot product, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{a}\\cdot \\mathbf{b} & ={a}_{x}{b}_{x}+{a}_{y}{b}_{y}+{a}_{z}{b}_{z} \\\\  & =(-2)\\cdot 3+5\\cdot x+1\\cdot (-4) \\\\  & =5x-10.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the dot product equal to $0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5x-10 & =0 \\\\ 5x & =10 \\\\ x & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299745 :question/problem "Differentiate ${e}^{x}(2+sin⁡(x)).$\n\n$({e}^{x}(2+sin⁡(x)))^{′}=$\n{{field-1}} $+\\,{e}^{x}cos⁡(x)$" :question/worked-solution "Here, we have the product of the two functions $u(x)={e}^{x}$ and $v(x)=2+sin⁡(x).$ So, we apply the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({e}^{x}sec⁡(x))^{′} & ={u}^{′}(x)\\cdot v(x)+u(x)\\cdot {v}^{′}(x) \\\\  & =({e}^{x})^{′}\\cdot (2+sin⁡(x))+{e}^{x}\\cdot (2+sin⁡(x))^{′} \\\\  & =({e}^{x})(2+sin⁡(x))+({e}^{x})(cos⁡(x)) \\\\  & =(2+sin⁡(x)){e}^{x}+{e}^{x}cos⁡(x)\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299746 :question/problem "Differentiate $2{x}^{2}cos⁡(x).$\n\n$(2{x}^{2}cos⁡(x))^{′}=$\n{{field-1}} $-\\,2{x}^{2}sin⁡(x)$" :question/worked-solution "Here, we have the product of the two functions $u(x)=2{x}^{2}$ and $v(x)=cos⁡(x).$ So, we apply the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2{x}^{2}cos⁡(x))^{′} & ={u}^{′}(x)\\cdot v(x)+u(x)\\cdot {v}^{′}(x) \\\\  & =(2{x}^{2})^{′}\\cdot cos⁡(x)+2{x}^{2}\\cdot (cos⁡(x))^{′} \\\\  & =(4x)(cos⁡(x))+(2{x}^{2})(-sin⁡(x)) \\\\  & =4xcos⁡(x)-2{x}^{2}sin⁡(x)\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299752 :question/problem "Differentiate $\\sqrt{x}tan⁡(x).$\n\n$(\\sqrt{x}tan⁡(x))^{′}=\\frac{tan⁡(x)}{2\\sqrt{x}}\\,+$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Here, we have the product of the two functions $u(x)=\\sqrt{x}$ and $v(x)=tan⁡(x).$ So, we apply the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\sqrt{x}tan⁡(x))^{′} & ={u}^{′}(x)\\cdot v(x)+u(x)\\cdot {v}^{′}(x) \\\\  & =(\\sqrt{x})^{′}\\cdot tan⁡(x)+\\sqrt{x}\\cdot (tan⁡(x))^{′} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x}}(tan⁡(x))+(\\sqrt{x})({sec}^{2}⁡(x)) \\\\  & =\\frac{tan⁡(x)}{2\\sqrt{x}}+\\sqrt{x}{sec}^{2}⁡(x)\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299759 :question/problem "Given that $f(x)={4}^{x}tan⁡(x),$ find ${f}^{′}(x).$\n\n${f}^{′}(x)={4}^{x}ln⁡(4)tan⁡(x)\\,+$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Here, we have the product of two functions $u(x)={4}^{x}$ and $v(x)=tan⁡(x).$ So, we apply the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & ={({4}^{x})}^{′}tan⁡(x)+{4}^{x}{(tan⁡(x))}^{′} \\\\  & =({4}^{x}ln⁡(4))tan⁡(x)+{4}^{x}({sec}^{2}⁡(x)) \\\\  & ={4}^{x}ln⁡(4)tan⁡(x)+{4}^{x}{sec}^{2}⁡(x)\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299797 :question/problem "If a curve $\\mathcal{C}$ is given by $y=(x+2)cos⁡x,$ then the slope of the tangent line to the curve at the point where $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ is{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "We differentiate using the product rule. Let $u(x)=x+2$ and $v(x)=cos⁡x.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=1,\\,\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=-sin⁡x.\n$$\n\nApplying the product rule gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =(x+2)\\cdot (-sin⁡x)+cos⁡x\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =-(x+2)sin⁡x+cos⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the slope of the tangent line, we now substitute $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ into the formula and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}} & =-(\\frac{\\pi }{2}+2)sin⁡\\frac{\\pi }{2}+cos⁡\\frac{\\pi }{2} \\\\  & =-(\\frac{\\pi }{2}+2)(1)+0 \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\pi }{2}-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent line is $-\\frac{\\pi }{2}-2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299798 :question/problem "If a curve $\\mathcal{C}$ is given by $y=(x-1)ln⁡(4x-3),$ calculate the slope of the tangent line to the curve at the point where $x=1$ is{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "We differentiate using the product rule. Let $u(x)=x-1$ and $v(x)=ln⁡(4x-3).$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=1,\\,\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{4}{4x-3}.\n$$\n\nApplying the product rule gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =(x-1)\\cdot \\frac{4}{4x-3}+ln⁡(4x-3).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the slope of the tangent line, we now substitute $x=1$ into the formula and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=1} & =(1-1)\\cdot \\frac{4}{4(1)-3}+ln⁡(4(1)-3) \\\\  & =0+ln⁡(1)=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent line is $0.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299799 :question/problem "If a curve $\\mathcal{C}$ is given by $y=({x}^{2}-4x+7){e}^{x},$ calculate the slope of the tangent line to the curve at the point where $x=0$ is{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "We differentiate using the product rule. Let $u(x)={x}^{2}-4x+7$ and $v(x)={e}^{x}.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=2x-4,\\,\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}={e}^{x}.\n$$\n\nApplying the product rule gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =({x}^{2}-4x+7)\\cdot {e}^{x}+{e}^{x}\\cdot (2x-4) \\\\  & ={e}^{x}({x}^{2}-2x+3).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the slope of the tangent line, we now substitute $x=0$ into the formula and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=0} & ={{e}^{x}({x}^{2}-2x+3)|}_{x=0} \\\\  & =1\\cdot (0-0+3)=3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent line is $3.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299804 :question/problem "Given that $f(x)={x}^{3}{e}^{x},$ find ${f}^{′}(x).$\n\n${f}^{′}(x)=$\n{{field-1}} $+\\,{x}^{3}{e}^{x}$" :question/worked-solution "Here, we have the product of two functions $u(x)={x}^{3}$ and $v(x)={e}^{x}.$ So, we apply the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =({x}^{3})^{′}{e}^{x}+{x}^{3}({e}^{x})^{′} \\\\  & =(3{x}^{2})({e}^{x})+({x}^{3})({e}^{x}) \\\\  & =3{x}^{2}{e}^{x}+{x}^{3}{e}^{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299805 :question/problem "Given that $f(x)=5{x}^{2}{e}^{x},$ find ${f}^{′}(x).$\n\n${f}^{′}(x)=10x{e}^{x}\\,+$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Here, we have the product of two functions $u(x)=5{x}^{2}$ and $v(x)={e}^{x}.$ So, we apply the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =(5{x}^{2})^{′}{e}^{x}+5{x}^{2}({e}^{x})^{′} \\\\  & =(10x)({e}^{x})+(5{x}^{2})({e}^{x}) \\\\  & =10x{e}^{x}+5{x}^{2}{e}^{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299906 :question/problem "Given that $f(x)={3}^{x}{log}_{3}⁡(x),$ find ${f}^{′}(x).$\n\n${f}^{′}(x)=$\n{{field-1}} $+\\,\\frac{{3}^{x}}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)}$" :question/worked-solution "Here, we have the product of the two functions $u(x)={3}^{x}$ and $v(x)={log}_{3}⁡(x).$ So, we apply the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & ={u}^{′}(x)\\cdot v(x)+u(x)\\cdot {v}^{′}(x) \\\\  & ={({3}^{x})}^{′}{log}_{3}⁡(x)+{3}^{x}{({log}_{3}⁡(x))}^{′} \\\\  & =({3}^{x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3))({log}_{3}⁡(x))+({3}^{x})\\left(\\frac{1}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)}\\right) \\\\  & ={3}^{x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3){log}_{3}⁡(x)+\\frac{{3}^{x}}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299907 :question/problem "Given that $f(x)={4}^{x}{log}_{4}⁡(x),$ find ${f}^{′}(x).$\n\n${f}^{′}(x)={4}^{x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4){log}_{4}⁡(x)\\,+$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Here, we have the product of the two functions $u(x)={4}^{x}$ and $v(x)={log}_{4}⁡(x).$ So, we apply the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & ={u}^{′}(x)\\cdot v(x)+u(x)\\cdot {v}^{′}(x) \\\\  & ={({4}^{x})}^{′}{log}_{4}⁡(x)+{4}^{x}{({log}_{4}⁡(x))}^{′} \\\\  & =({4}^{x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4))({log}_{4}⁡(x))+({4}^{x})\\left(\\frac{1}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4)}\\right) \\\\  & ={4}^{x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4){log}_{4}⁡(x)+\\frac{{4}^{x}}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4)}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299908 :question/problem "Given that $f(x)={5}^{-x}{log}_{7}⁡(x),$ find ${f}^{′}(x).$\n\n${f}^{′}(x)=$\n{{field-1}} $+\\,\\frac{{5}^{-x}}{xln⁡(7)}$" :question/worked-solution "Here, we have the product of the two functions $u(x)={5}^{-x}$ and $v(x)={log}_{7}⁡(x).$ So, we apply the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & ={u}^{′}(x)\\cdot v(x)+u(x)\\cdot {v}^{′}(x) \\\\  & ={({5}^{-x})}^{′}{log}_{7}⁡(x)+{5}^{-x}{({log}_{7}⁡(x))}^{′} \\\\  & =(-{5}^{-x}ln⁡(5))({log}_{7}⁡(x))+({5}^{-x})\\left(\\frac{1}{xln⁡(7)}\\right) \\\\  & =-{5}^{-x}ln⁡(5){log}_{7}⁡(x)+\\frac{{5}^{-x}}{xln⁡(7)}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299913 :question/problem "Consider the curve defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=t-2,\\,\\,\\,y={t}^{2}-4,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nThe Cartesian equation of this curve is\n\n$y=$\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "To find the Cartesian equation, we eliminate $t.$ First, we use the $x$ equation to solve for $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=t-2\\,⇒\\,t=x+2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, substituting $t=x+2$ into our expression for $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =(x+2)^{2}-4 \\\\ y & ={x}^{2}+4x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the Cartesian equation of the given curve is \n$y={x}^{2}+4x.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299919 :question/problem "Consider the curve defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{1}{t+3},\\,\\,\\,y=\\frac{t}{-2t+1},\\,t\\ne -3,\\,\\,\\,t\\ne \\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nThe Cartesian equation of this curve is\n\n$y=$\n{{field-1}}$,\\,x\\ne $\n{{field-2}}$,\\,\\,\\,x\\ne 0.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the Cartesian equation, we eliminate $t.$ First, we use the $x$ equation to solve for $t:$\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{1}{t+3}\\,⇒\\,t=-3+\\frac{1}{x}\n$$\n\nNow, substituting $t=-3+\\frac{1}{x}$ into our expression for $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{(-3+\\frac{1}{x})}{(-2\\,(-3+\\frac{1}{x})+1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-3+\\frac{1}{x})}{(6-\\frac{2}{x}+1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-3+\\frac{1}{x})}{(7-\\frac{2}{x})} \\\\  & =\\frac{-3x+1}{\\cancel{x}}\\cdot \\frac{\\cancel{x}}{7x-2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1-3x}{7x-2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the Cartesian equation of the given curve is $y=\\frac{1-3x}{7x-2}.$\n\nFinally, since $t\\ne \\frac{1}{2}$ and $x=\\frac{1}{t+3}$ has range $x\\ne 0,$ we must have \n$x\\ne \\frac{2}{7},\\text{ }0.$ \n\nTherefore, the complete Cartesian equation is\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{1-3x}{7x-2},\\,x\\ne \\frac{2}{7},\\,\\,\\,x\\ne 0.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299922 :question/problem "Consider the curve defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,\\,\\,y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-2,\\,t\\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nThe Cartesian equation of this curve is\n\n{{field-1}}$=1.$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's make the trigonometric functions the subject of each equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t=\\frac{x}{3} \\\\ y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-2\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t=y+2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the Pythagorean identity ${\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}t+{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}t=1$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\left(\\frac{x}{3}\\right)}^{2}+(y+2)^{2} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{1}{9}{x}^{2}+(y+2)^{2} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299923 :question/problem "Find the Cartesian equation of the curve defined parametrically by\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{t+1}{3},\\,\\,\\,y=({t}^{2}-4)(1-t)^{2},\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\n$y=$\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "To find the Cartesian equation, we eliminate $t.$ First, we use the $x$ equation to solve for $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\frac{t+1}{3}\\,⇒\\,t=3x-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, substituting $t=3x-1$ into our expression for $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =((3x-1)^{2}-4){(1-(3x-1))}^{2} \\\\  & =(9{x}^{2}-6x+1-4)(2-3x)^{2} \\\\  & =(9{x}^{2}-6x-3)(2-3x)^{2} \\\\  & =3(3{x}^{2}-2x-1)(2-3x)^{2} \\\\  & =3(3x+1)(x-1)(2-3x)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the Cartesian equation of the given curve is $y=3(3x+1)(x-1)(2-3x)^{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299924 :question/problem "Find the Cartesian equation of the curve defined parametrically by\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{1-t}{4},\\,\\,\\,y=({t}^{2}-1)(1+t)^{2},\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\n$y=$\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "To find the Cartesian equation, we eliminate $t.$ First, we use the $x$ equation to solve for $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\frac{1-t}{4}\\,⇒\\,t=1-4x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, substituting $t=1-4x$ into our expression for $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =((1-4x)^{2}-1)((1+(1-4x))^{2}) \\\\  & =(1-8x+16{x}^{2}-1)((2-4x)^{2}) \\\\  & =(-8x+16{x}^{2})(4)(1-2x)^{2} \\\\  & =8x(2x-1)(2-4x)^{2} \\\\  & =8x(2x-1)\\cdot 4(1-2x)^{2} \\\\  & =32x(2x-1)^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the Cartesian equation of the given curve is $y=32x(2x-1)^{3}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299925 :question/problem "Consider the curve defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=\\sqrt{\\,t+1\\,},\\,\\,\\,y=t(4-t),\\,t\\ge 0.\n$$\n\nThe Cartesian equation of this curve is\n\n$y=$\n{{field-1}}$,\\,x\\ge 1.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the Cartesian equation, we eliminate $t.$ First, we use the $x$ equation to solve for $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\sqrt{\\,t+1\\,}\\,⇒\\,t={x}^{2}-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, substituting $t={x}^{2}-1$ into our expression for $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =({x}^{2}-1)(4-({x}^{2}-1)) \\\\  & =({x}^{2}-1)(5-{x}^{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the Cartesian equation of the given curve is $y=({x}^{2}-1)(5-{x}^{2}).$\n\nFinally, since $t\\ge 0$ and $t={x}^{2}-1,$ we must have ${x}^{2}-1\\ge 0,$ i.e., $x\\ge 1.$\n\nTherefore, the complete Cartesian equation is $y=({x}^{2}-1)(5-{x}^{2}),\\,x\\ge 1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299926 :question/problem "Consider the curve defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=\\sqrt{{t}^{2}-9},\\,\\,\\,y=3{t}^{2},\\,t\\ge 3.\n$$\n\nThe Cartesian equation of this curve is\n\n$y=$\n{{field-1}}$,\\,x\\ge 0.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the Cartesian equation, we eliminate $t.$ First, we use the $x$ equation to solve for ${t}^{2}:$\n\n$$\nx=\\sqrt{{t}^{2}-9}\\,⇒\\,{t}^{2}={x}^{2}+9\n$$\n\nNow, substituting ${t}^{2}={x}^{2}+9$ into our expression for $y,$ we get\n\n$$\ny=3({x}^{2}+9)=3{x}^{2}+27.\n$$\n\nSo, the Cartesian equation of the given curve is $y=3{x}^{2}+27.$\n\nFinally, since $t\\ge 3$ and $x=\\sqrt{{t}^{2}-9},$ we must have $x\\ge 0.$\n\nTherefore, the complete Cartesian equation is $y=3{x}^{2}+27,\\,x\\ge 0.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299929 :question/problem "Consider the curve defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{1}{2t+1},\\,\\,\\,y=(t+1)^{2},\\,t\\ne -\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nThe Cartesian equation of this curve is\n\n$y=$\n{{field-1}}$,\\,x\\ne $\n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "To find the Cartesian equation, we eliminate $t.$ First, we use the $x$ equation to solve for $t:$\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{1}{2t+1}\\,⇒\\,2t+1=\\frac{1}{x}\\,⇒\\,t=\\frac{1}{2x}-\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, substituting $t=\\frac{1}{2x}-\\frac{1}{2}$ into our expression for $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & ={(\\frac{1}{2x}-\\frac{1}{2}+1)}^{2} \\\\  & ={(\\frac{1}{2x}+\\frac{1}{2})}^{2} \\\\  & ={\\left(\\frac{1+x}{2x}\\right)}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{(1+x)^{2}}{4{x}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the Cartesian equation of the given curve is $y=\\frac{(1+x)^{2}}{4{x}^{2}}.$\n\nFinally, since $t\\ne -\\frac{1}{2}$ and $x=\\frac{1}{2t+1}$ we must have $x\\ne 0.$\n\nTherefore, the complete Cartesian equation is\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{(1+x)^{2}}{4{x}^{2}},\\,x\\ne 0.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302048 :question/problem "Consider the curve defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,\\,\\,y=-6\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t,\\,t\\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nThe Cartesian equation of this curve is\n\n$y=$\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "First, let's make the trigonometric functions the subject of each equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t=\\frac{x}{5} \\\\ y=-6\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t=-\\frac{y}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the double-angle formula for cosine and the Pythagorean identity, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}2t & ={\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}t-{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}t \\\\  & ={\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}t-(1-{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}t) \\\\  & =2{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}t-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\operatorname{cos}⁡t=\\frac{x}{5}$ and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t=-\\frac{y}{6}$ into this formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{y}{6} & =2{\\left(\\frac{x}{5}\\right)}^{2}-1 \\\\ -\\frac{y}{6} & =\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{25}-1 \\\\ y & =-6(\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{25}-1) \\\\ y & =-\\frac{12{x}^{2}}{25}+6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the Cartesian equation of the given curve is $y=-\\frac{12{x}^{2}}{25}+6.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302049 :question/problem "Consider the curve defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,\\,\\,y=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t,\\,t\\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nThe Cartesian equation of this curve is\n\n$y=$\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "First, let's make the trigonometric functions the subject of each equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t=\\frac{x}{6} \\\\ y=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t=\\frac{y}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the double-angle formula for cosine and the Pythagorean identity, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}2t & ={\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}t-{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}t \\\\  & ={\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}t-(1-{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}t) \\\\  & =2{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}t-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\operatorname{cos}⁡t=\\frac{x}{6}$ and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t=\\frac{y}{5}$ into this formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y}{5} & =2{\\left(\\frac{x}{6}\\right)}^{2}-1 \\\\ \\frac{y}{5} & =2\\left(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{36}\\right)-1 \\\\ \\frac{y}{5} & =\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{36}-1 \\\\ y & =5(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{18}-1) \\\\ y & =\\frac{5{x}^{2}}{18}-5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the Cartesian equation of the given curve is $y=\\frac{5{x}^{2}}{18}-5.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302062 :question/problem "Consider the curve defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=-3t+2,\\,\\,\\,y=-\\frac{1}{t},\\,t\\ne 0.\n$$\n\nThe Cartesian equation of this curve is\n\n$y=$\n{{field-1}}$,\\,x\\ne $\n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "To find the Cartesian equation, we eliminate $t.$ First, we use the $x$ equation to solve for $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=-3t+2\\,⇒\\,t=\\frac{2-x}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, substituting $t=\\frac{2-x}{3}$ into our expression for $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-\\frac{1}{\\left(\\frac{2-x}{3}\\right)} \\\\ y & =\\frac{3}{x-2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the Cartesian equation of the given curve is $y=\\frac{3}{x-2}.$\n\nFinally, since $t\\ne 0$ and $x=-3t+2$ , we must have $x\\ne 2.$ \n\nTherefore, the complete Cartesian equation is\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{3}{x-2},\\,x\\ne 2.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302089 :question/problem "![](images/b5/b51c85930a5b93bcf7f3e7537cd39cf4134d93470b580c0f7276ddb58ade7297.png)\n\nConsider the circle of radius $4$ centered at the origin, as shown above. Construct a set of parametric equations, defined for $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ),$ that traverse the circle once in the counterclockwise direction starting at the point $A(4,0).$\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}$,\\,y=$\n{{field-2}}$,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )$" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of a circle of radius $r$ centered at the origin are\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nUnder this parametrization, the circle is traversed once in the counterclockwise direction, starting from $(r,0).$\n\nIn our case, the radius is $r=4.$ Substituting $r=4$ gives\n\n$$\nx=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302090 :question/problem "![](images/da/da3a33a3ade10663064516bac22c07459ccf7ce556753ba94d70ee832e947458.png)\n\nConsider the circle of radius $10$ centered at the origin, as shown above. Construct a set of parametric equations, defined for $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ),$ that traverse the circle once in the counterclockwise direction starting at the point $A(10,0).$\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}$,\\,y=$\n{{field-2}}$,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )$" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of a circle of radius $r$ centered at the origin are\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nUnder this parametrization, the circle is traversed once in the counterclockwise direction, starting from $(r,0).$\n\nIn our case, the radius is $r=10.$ Substituting $r=10$ gives\n\n$$\nx=10\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=10\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302096 :question/problem "![](images/46/460b94dd8d68c7ed7e5bab45befb9c86496291ae7f192ab022e47493807525d5.png)\n\nConsider the circle of radius $5$ centered at $(-3,4)$, as shown above. Construct a set of parametric equations, defined for $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ),$ that traverse the circle once in the counterclockwise direction starting at the point $A(2,4).$\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}$,\\,y=$\n{{field-2}}$,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )$" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of a circle of radius $r$ centered at the point $(a,b)$ are\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +a,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +b,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nUnder this parametrization, the circle is traversed once in the counterclockwise direction, starting from $(r+a,b).$\n\nIn our case, we have a circle of radius $5$ centered at the point $(-3,4).$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the circle are\n\n$$\nx=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -3,\\,y=5\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +4,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302097 :question/problem "![](images/59/59cef258a1ad0e6a0f56c37e0b7020e5341c329d5655c41f49e6e78eafca8b3f.png)\n\nConsider the circle of radius $6$ centered at $(-5,-2)$, as shown above. Construct a set of parametric equations, defined for $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ),$ that traverse the circle once in the counterclockwise direction starting at the point $A(1,-2).$\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}$,\\,y=$\n{{field-2}}$,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )$" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of a circle of radius $r$ centered at the point $(a,b)$ are\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +a,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +b,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nUnder this parametrization, the circle is traversed once in the counterclockwise direction, starting from $(r+a,b).$\n\nIn our case, we have a circle of radius $6$ centered at the point $(-5,-2).$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the circle are\n\n$$\nx=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -5,\\,y=6\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -2,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302098 :question/problem "![](images/11/11f1e6651d86e0b00884091338029395cd6d2ff44f25b97d010ec750d13bef59.png)\n\nThe arc of a circle above is defined by the parametric equations \n\n$$\nx=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nWhat is the domain of the parameter $\\theta ?$ Assume $\\theta $ is between $0$ and $2\\pi .$\n\n$\\theta \\in [${{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$]$" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of a circle  of radius $r$  centered at  the origin are\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, the radius $r=4.$ So, the parametric equations of the *full* circle are \n\n$$\nx=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nTo restrict the parameterization to the arc above, we associate the parameter $\\theta $ with the central angle formed by a point $(x,y)$ on the circle and the $x$-axis.\n\n![](images/2a/2a281b909e4a3577ea3774e9d75c43d97e4d4a69d0f673eed4189834e1ed1c43.png)\n\nWe can see that if we restrict $\\theta $ to $[0,\\frac{7\\pi }{6}]$, we get the required arc of the circle.\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the arc of the circle are \n\n$$\nx=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,\\frac{7\\pi }{6}].\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302099 :question/problem "![](images/ed/ed6f528f7e2b873812d492cbeec386ab56af626a964e98b2929824ec9075d320.png)\n\nThe quarter-circle above is defined by the parametric equations \n\n$$\nx=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nWhat is the domain of the parameter $\\theta ?$ Assume $\\theta $ is between $0$ and $2\\pi .$\n\n$\\theta \\in [${{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$]$" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of a circle  of radius $r$  centered at  the origin are\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, the radius $r=4.$ So, the parametric equations of the *full* circle are \n\n$$\nx=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nTo restrict the parameterization to the arc above, we associate the parameter $\\theta $ with the central angle formed by a point $(x,y)$ on the circle and the $x$-axis.\n\n![](images/68/682f73ccec945de2789ecca71aa6dae650c485189c60ffc64ab7a7aa108665da.png)\n\nWe can see that if we restrict $\\theta $ to $[0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}]$, we get the required arc of the circle.\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the arc of the circle are \n\n$$\nx=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}].\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302102 :question/problem "![](images/fb/fbe6faf7f14f9ce51c299f18f9596c08c650079d2ca23398180baff8ea965553.png)\n\nThe three-quarter-circle above is defined by the parametric equations \n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -1,\\,y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +1.\n$$\n\nWhat is the domain of the parameter $\\theta ?$ Assume $\\theta $ is between $0$ and $2\\pi .$\n\n$\\theta \\in [${{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$]$" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of a circle of radius $r$ centered at the point $(a,b)$ are\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +a,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +b,\\,\\theta \\in (0,2\\pi ].\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have a circle of radius $1$ centered at the point $(-1,1).$ So, the parametric equations of the *full* circle are \n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -1,\\,y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +1,\\,\\theta \\in (0,2\\pi ].\n$$\n\nTo restrict the parameterization to a three-quarter-circle, we associate the parameter $\\theta $ with the central angle formed by a point $(x,y)$ on the circle and the line parallel to the $x$-axis and crossing the center of the circle. \n\n![](images/28/28d5eaee4de3a6100a92fa017b71d9860ea19cd7073284b41511dda4155ecb6d.png)\n\nWe can see that if we restrict $\\theta $ to $[\\frac{\\pi }{2},2\\pi ]$, we get the required three-quarter-circle.\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the three-quarter circle are \n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -1,\\,y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +1,\\,\\theta \\in [\\frac{\\pi }{2},2\\pi ].\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302103 :question/problem "The Cartesian equation of the circle defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -3,\\,y=5\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +4,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi \n$$\n\nis given by\n\n$(x\\,+$\n{{field-1}}$)^{2}+(y\\,-$\n{{field-2}}$)^{2}=$\n{{field-3}}." :question/worked-solution "To find the Cartesian equation of the circle, we need to eliminate the parameter $\\theta .$ We can do this using the Pythagorean theorem.\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ term in the $x$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x+3}{5} & =\\operatorname{cos}\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(x+3)^{2}}{{5}^{2}} & ={\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(x+3)^{2}}{25} & ={\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ term in the $y$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y-4}{5} & =\\operatorname{sin}\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(y-4)^{2}}{{5}^{2}} & ={\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(y-4)^{2}}{25} & ={\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the two equations and using ${\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta +{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta =1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x+3)^{2}}{25}+\\frac{(y-4)^{2}}{25} & ={\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta +{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(x+3)^{2}}{25}+\\frac{(y-4)^{2}}{25} & =1 \\\\ (x+3)^{2}+(y-4)^{2} & =25\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian equation is $(x+3)^{2}+(y-4)^{2}=25.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302104 :question/problem "The Cartesian equation of the circle defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{5}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -3,\\,y=\\frac{5}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +1,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi \n$$\n\nis given by\n\n$(x\\,+$\n{{field-1}}$)^{2}+(y\\,-$\n{{field-2}}$)^{2}=$\n{{field-3}}." :question/worked-solution "To find the Cartesian equation of the circle, we need to eliminate the parameter $\\theta .$ We can do this using the Pythagorean theorem.\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ term in the $x$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+3 & =\\frac{5}{2}\\operatorname{cos}\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{2}{5}(x+3) & =\\operatorname{cos}\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{4}{25}{(x+3)}^{2} & ={\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ term in the $y$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-1 & =\\frac{5}{2}\\operatorname{sin}\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{2}{5}(y-1) & =\\operatorname{sin}\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{4}{25}{(y-1)}^{2} & ={\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the two equations and using ${\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta +{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta =1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{25}{(x+3)}^{2}+\\frac{4}{25}{(y-1)}^{2} & ={\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta +{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{4}{25}{(x+3)}^{2}+\\frac{4}{25}{(y-1)}^{2} & =1 \\\\ {(x+3)}^{2}+{(y-1)}^{2} & =\\frac{25}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian equation is ${(x+3)}^{2}+{(y-1)}^{2}=\\frac{25}{4}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302105 :question/problem "The Cartesian equation of the circle defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\frac{7}{2},\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -\\frac{11}{4},\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi \n$$\n\nis given by\n\n$(x\\,-$\n{{field-1}}$)^{2}+(y\\,+$\n{{field-2}}$)^{2}=$\n{{field-3}}." :question/worked-solution "To find the Cartesian equation of the circle, we need to eliminate the parameter $\\theta .$ We can do this using the Pythagorean theorem.\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ term in the $x$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-\\frac{7}{2} & =3\\operatorname{cos}\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}(x-\\frac{7}{2}) & =\\operatorname{cos}\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{1}{9}{(x-\\frac{7}{2})}^{2} & ={\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ term in the $y$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y+\\frac{11}{4} & =3\\operatorname{sin}\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}(y+\\frac{11}{4}) & =\\operatorname{sin}\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{1}{9}{(y+\\frac{11}{4})}^{2} & ={\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the two equations and using ${\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta +{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta =1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{9}{(x-\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}+\\frac{1}{9}{(y+\\frac{11}{4})}^{2} & ={\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta +{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{1}{9}{(x-\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}+\\frac{1}{9}{(y+\\frac{11}{4})}^{2} & =1 \\\\ {(x-\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}+{(y+\\frac{11}{4})}^{2} & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian equation is $$\n\\displaystyle {(x-\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}+{(y+\\frac{11}{4})}^{2}=9.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302106 :question/problem "The Cartesian equation of the circle defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=7\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -5,\\,y=7\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +13,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi \n$$\n\nis given by\n\n$(x\\,+$\n{{field-1}}$)^{2}+(y\\,-$\n{{field-2}}$)^{2}=$\n{{field-3}}." :question/worked-solution "To find the Cartesian equation of the circle, we need to eliminate the parameter $\\theta .$ We can do this using the Pythagorean theorem.\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ term in the $x$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+5 & =7\\operatorname{cos}\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(x+5)^{2}}{{7}^{2}} & ={\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{(x+5)}^{2}}{49} & ={\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ term in the $y$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-13 & =7\\operatorname{sin}\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(y-13)^{2}}{{7}^{2}} & =\\operatorname{sin}\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{(y-13)}^{2}}{49} & ={\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the two equations and using ${\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta +{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta =1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{(x+5)}^{2}}{49}+\\frac{{(y-13)}^{2}}{49} & ={\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta +{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{(x+5)}^{2}}{49}+\\frac{{(y-13)}^{2}}{49} & =1 \\\\ {(x+5)}^{2}+{(y-13)}^{2} & =49.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian equation is ${(x+5)}^{2}+{(y-13)}^{2}=49.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302170 :question/problem "Differentiate $2\\mathbf{g}(t)-\\mathbf{h}(t)$ with respect to $t$, where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{g}(t) & =⟨\\operatorname{cos}t,\\hspace{0.222em}3t-3,\\hspace{0.222em}-{t}^{2}⟩ \\\\ \\mathbf{h}(t) & =⟨\\operatorname{sin}t,\\hspace{0.222em}-t,\\hspace{0.222em}4⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Using the constant multiple and the sum rules, we have\n\n$$\n(2\\mathbf{g}(t)-\\mathbf{h}(t))^{'}=2{\\mathbf{g}}^{'}(t)-{\\mathbf{h}}^{'}(t)\n$$\n\nLet's now find the derivatives of each function.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{g}}^{'}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\operatorname{cos}t,\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3t-3),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-{t}^{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-\\operatorname{sin}t,\\hspace{0.222em}3,\\hspace{0.222em}-2t⟩ \\\\ {\\mathbf{h}}^{'}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\operatorname{sin}t,\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-t),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}4⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\operatorname{cos}t,\\hspace{0.222em}-1,\\hspace{0.222em}0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2\\mathbf{g}(t)-\\mathbf{h}(t))^{'} & =2{\\mathbf{g}}^{'}(t)-{\\mathbf{h}}^{'}(t) \\\\  & =2⟨-\\operatorname{sin}t,\\hspace{0.222em}3,\\hspace{0.222em}-2t⟩-⟨\\operatorname{cos}t,\\hspace{0.222em}-1,\\hspace{0.222em}0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-2\\operatorname{sin}t,\\hspace{0.222em}6,\\hspace{0.222em}-4t⟩-⟨\\operatorname{cos}t,\\hspace{0.222em}-1,\\hspace{0.222em}0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-2\\operatorname{sin}t-\\operatorname{cos}t,\\hspace{0.222em}6-(-1),\\hspace{0.222em}-4t-0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-2\\operatorname{sin}t-\\operatorname{cos}t,\\hspace{0.222em}7,\\hspace{0.222em}-4t⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302171 :question/problem "If  $\\mathbf{f}(u)=u\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+2u\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+3u\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}$ and $u(t)={t}^{2}$, then $(\\mathbf{f}∘u)^{'}(t)=$" :question/worked-solution "Using the chain rule, we can write \n\n$$\n(\\mathbf{f}∘u)^{'}(t)={\\mathbf{f}}^{'}(u(t)){u}^{'}(t)\n$$\n\nNow, let's find the derivatives of each function.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{f}}^{'}(u) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}u}u\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}u}2u\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}3u\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =\\mathbf{i}+2\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+3\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k} \\\\ {u}^{'}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}) \\\\  & =2t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\mathbf{f}∘u)^{'}(t) & =\\mathbf{f}'(u(t))u'(t) \\\\  & =(\\mathbf{i}+2\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+3\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k})2t \\\\  & =2t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+4t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+6t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302172 :question/problem "Differentiate $\\mathbf{f}(t)+\\mathbf{g}(t)+\\mathbf{h}(t)$ with respect to $t$, where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{f}(t) & =⟨-1,\\hspace{0.222em}0,\\hspace{0.222em}1⟩ \\\\ \\mathbf{g}(t) & =⟨-t,\\hspace{0.222em}0,t⟩ \\\\ \\mathbf{h}(t) & =⟨-{t}^{2},0,{t}^{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Using the sum rule, we can write\n\n$$\n(\\mathbf{f}(t)+\\mathbf{g}(t)+\\mathbf{h}(t))^{'}={\\mathbf{f}}^{'}(t)+{\\mathbf{g}}^{'}(t)+{\\mathbf{h}}^{'}(t)\n$$\n\nLet's now find the derivatives of each function.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{f}}^{'}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-1),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(0),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,0,0⟩ \\\\ {\\mathbf{g}}^{'}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-t),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(0),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-1,0,1⟩ \\\\ {\\mathbf{h}}^{'}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-{t}^{2}),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(0),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-2t,\\hspace{0.222em}0,\\hspace{0.222em}2t⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\mathbf{f}(t)+\\mathbf{g}(t)+\\mathbf{h}(t))^{'} & ={\\mathbf{f}}^{'}(t)+{\\mathbf{g}}^{'}(t)+{\\mathbf{h}}^{'}(t) \\\\  & =⟨0,0,0⟩+⟨-1,0,1⟩+⟨-2t,\\hspace{0.222em}0,\\hspace{0.222em}2t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-2t-1,\\hspace{0.222em}0,\\hspace{0.222em}2t+1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302269 :question/problem "Consider the curve with parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{3}{2}t+1,\\,y=-\\frac{5}{4}t+2,\\,-1\\le t\\le 4.\n$$\n\nFill in the blanks in the following table of values:\n\n| **$t$** | $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | {{field-1}} | $1$ | $\\frac{5}{2}$ | $4$ | {{field-2}} | $7$ |\n| $y$ | {{field-3}} | $2$ | $\\frac{3}{4}$ | $-\\frac{1}{2}$ | $-\\frac{7}{4}$ | $-3$ |" :question/worked-solution "- To find the value of $x(-1),$ we substitute $t=-1$ into $x=\\frac{3}{2}t+1.$ This gives\n\n$$\nx(-1)=\\frac{3}{2}(-1)+1=-\\frac{3}{2}+\\frac{2}{2}=-\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\n- To find the value of $y(-1),$ we substitute $t=-1$ into $y=-\\frac{5}{4}t+2.$ This gives\n\n$$\ny(-1)=-\\frac{5}{4}(-1)+2=\\frac{5}{4}+\\frac{8}{4}=\\frac{13}{4}.\n$$\n\n- To find the value of $x(3),$ we substitute $t=3$ into $x=\\frac{3}{2}t+1.$ This gives\n\n$$\nx(2)=\\frac{3}{2}(3)+1=\\frac{9}{2}+\\frac{2}{2}=\\frac{11}{2}.\n$$\n\nThen the table is \n\n \n\n| **$t$** | $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $-\\frac{1}{2}$ | $1$ | $\\frac{5}{2}$ | $4$ | $\\frac{11}{2}$ | $7$ |\n| $y$ | $\\frac{13}{4}$ | $2$ | $\\frac{3}{4}$ | $-\\frac{1}{2}$ | $-\\frac{7}{4}$ | $-3$ |"}]}
